WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
Now I am sure you have seen in the latest wave of PSA's imploring you

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.520
not to eat a Tide pod

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.720
Well I'm not here to tell you not to eat a Tide pod

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.900
I'm just gonna tell what to expect if you try...

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.320
You see the pods outer layer is a plastic wrapping that puts its contents under pressure

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.940
so you don't have too bite all that hard for the gel inside to explode out

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.720
If the gel does indeed come into contact with the insides of your mouth

00:00:21.725 --> 00:00:23.755
which is kinda the point in all this...

00:00:23.755 --> 00:00:26.795
its low PH levels will begin killing any cells

00:00:26.795 --> 00:00:29.335
it comes into contact with immediately

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.325
And when cells die they don't just VANISH

00:00:32.325 --> 00:00:35.140
they leave behind their remains...leaving your mouth filled with dead cells

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.540
and damaged tissue

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.440
as the gel causes an immediate burning sensation.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.340
A burning sensation that will be AGONIZINGLY painful!

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.840
The gel of the pod is thick, and because of how thick and sticky it is

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.960
if some of it goes down your throat, it can become stuck or lodged

00:00:49.965 --> 00:00:53.560
in your esophagus, killing even more cells as it goes.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.340
This, in turn, will kick your immune system in high gear

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:57.860
resulting in swelling.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.920
That swelling of your esophagus can also affect the

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.780
muscles that control your breathing.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.800
Meaning you just might find yourself choking

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.480
even if you do manage to vomit up the detergent.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.760
If you managed to survive the Tide Pod challenge by not choking to death

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.500
Congratulations! You now get the joy of spending

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.400
several days with the lingering pain of chemical burns

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.460
inside of your body.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.060
Now for those of you who fancy yourselves

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.140
hardcore enough for a REAL challenge

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.800
I've got two words for ya.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:24.840
Chubby

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:25.680
Bunny.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.220
*intro*

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.760
*Trigger Warning - this episode contains mentions of suicide*

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.440
Hello internet! Welcome to Film Theory!

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.640
Where today we're talking about dumb news.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.120
And by dumb news, I don't mean people chomping

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.220
on Tide Pods,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.880
I'm talking about the media that gobbled

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.840
up this story faster than an unattended 6 year old

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.240
with brightly colored detergent packets.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.280
As the story goes, sometime in late December 2017

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.820
Several viral tweets brought up the idea of eating Tide Pods like candy.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.160
The biggest being this thread with everyone's favorite

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.380
lunchtime snack Gushers, trying to get them make a Tide Pod themed flavor

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.180
Now, this was far from the first time the idea of

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:19.000
detergent packet equals candy was brought up

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.120
But it was the first time that people actually took it seriously.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.160
Causing a supposed epidemic sweeping through the internet as kids across the country

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.720
started filming themselves cramming laundry detergent

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.640
into their mouths. The internet began mocking the trend

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.940
in its usual fashion,  numerous businesses moved

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.980
to cash in, YouTube and Facebook began banning

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.980
Tidepod videos, and Tide released what turned out to be

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.960
one of the most baffling PSAs of all time.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.995
And, of course, there was requisite mainstream media coverage

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.885
wagging their fingers at teenagers across the country, saying

00:02:50.885 --> 00:02:53.160
"Can you believe kids these days?" or

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.740
"Oh, these stupid teenagers trying to find viral fame!"

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.720
Except they're completely wrong to blame the teenagers.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.340
This time, mainstream media is the one to blame.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.840
If you stop and look at the data,

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.660
it becomes clear that the story of this dangerous new trend online

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.200
was something that THEY created.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.520
They took a small handful of jokes and videos, and blew

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.520
it up into a fad that suddenly had

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.560
thousands of copycats.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.420
So don't be wagging your finger at "kids these days", news media!

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.540
You're the problem this time.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.200
And good ol' MatPat is about to show you why.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.840
A January 22nd press release from the American Association of Poison Control Center (AAPCC),

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.780
reported that between January 1st

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.560
and January 14th,

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:41.280
they received a total of 39 cases of teens intentionally exposing themselves to a liquid laundry packet

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.860
39 cases in 14 days.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.900
The following week, there were an additional 47 calls

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.220
In other words, we see a huge spike of the number

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:50.960
of Poisoning Control Center calls relating

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.940
to teens ingesting Tide Pod after January 14th.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.120
Now, remember that day

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.360
because it's not just an increasing calls after January 14

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.900
it's also in increase in videos.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:02.820
As I mentioned before,

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:06.160
most people claim that teens started munching on Tide Pod packets

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.600
in late December and through early January.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:10.680
But if you actually look at the numbers,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.420
that just isn't true.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.160
From December 1 through January 7,

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.200
There's only 242 Tide Pod videos

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.300
across all of Twitter, Instagram, YouTube and Facebook.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.480
Yeah, I checked them all.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.340
Now, that may seem like a lot,

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.420
but look at this:

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.880
Between the 7th and 14th,

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:30.160
there were 387 more.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.080
In one week, as opposed to 5 weeks.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.200
But, here's where the things start to get crazy.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.420
Between the 14th and the 21st,

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:41.680
1,502 new Tide Pod videos

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:42.520
get uploaded

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:43.820
In just 7 days!

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:48.460
Then next week, another 1,517 new videos.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.820
So anyway you look at the data,

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.080
it seems like between the 13th and 14th,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.460
something happened that caused the sudden spike

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.060
in Tide Pod related incidents.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.320
This period here, is when the trend

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.080
blew up.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.900
Now, you might think it was online influencers

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.500
talking about the trend that spread awareness of the problem,

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:04.220
But that's...

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.980
not definitely not the case here.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.820
The top Tide Pod video online belongs to the meme lord

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.020
"CAN YOUR CHAIR DO THIS?"

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:10.740
PewDiePie

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.060
whose video on the subject was released January 4th

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.540
Racking up most of its 6 MILLION views

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.340
in a matter of 5 days.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:21.340
If he were truly the one driving more videos to be made,

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.320
you would see an uptake in the aftermath of his video.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.580
But what you actually see

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.160
is the EXACT OPPOSITE.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.000
Looking at Tide Pod video uploads,

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.640
day by day, the trend was practically dead

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:32.900
around the 9th and 10th

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:36.120
having only around 20 videos uploaded each of these days

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.260
the lowest point that they had been for months.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:40.920
which means that for the first time ever! the media CAN'T

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.980
blame something on ya boii Pewds

00:05:42.985 --> 00:05:43.760
Truth be told

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:46.720
most of the internet was kinda over Tide pods by that point...

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:47.340
Nope!

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.960
This the news is gonna point the finger of blame at anyone,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.920
it has to be at THEMSELVES.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:55.720
January 12th is when Tide released their "No, No, No, No, No" PSA.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.900
The next day is when news organizations really started to pick up on this story,

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.200
prompting Google searches for 'Tide pod challenge' to hit an all time high.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.560
Increasing to 10x the amount of search traffic

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.000
relative to the previous week.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.120
And it's here when we see the uploads of videos labeled with 'Tide Pod' start to skyrocket.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:19.540
Reaching between 100 and 300 new uploads every. single. day.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:24.800
nearly 10x more uploads per day than when this "epidemic" was supposedly

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.380
so bad earlier in the month.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:29.660
The news media had created a mountain out of a molehill,

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.340
sparked hundreds of copy-cats,

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:35.840
and thus the dumbest and potentially most dangerous meme of 2018,

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:36.980
Now let me be clear,

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:38.940
I don't think that they any of this intentionally,

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.700
Truth be told, they're just not smart enough.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:46.300
For instance, after tide pods became a fad, a number of appeared posts on social media.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:50.080
people photographing stores with anti-theft measures in place for the detergent.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:55.420
People in the media found those posts and figured "Hey, it must be because of that crazy new challenge."

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.500
Except that there is a problem with those stories.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:02.560
Retailers like Walmart and Kroger have been keeping products locked up to prevent theft for years!

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:09.660
With news stories dating all the was back to 2012 reporting that laundry detergent is one of the markets' most shop lifted items

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:14.220
But hey, why bother to check the facts when we have these viral tweets to go off of.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:19.220
Surely these are 100% factually accurate!  The story writes itself.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:25.540
Basically, what I'm trying to say here is that, in their coverage of this meme, the news created a huge case of "copycat effect."

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:31.780
According to David Phillips, a sociologist at the University of California at San Diego, the copycat effect goes as follows:

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:37.380
"When I am waiting at a traffic light, and the light is red, I wonder sometimes if I should just cross and J-Walk."

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:44.240
"Then someone else does it, and so I do it too. I'm getting permission to act from someone else who is engaging in a deviant act."

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:50.460
In other words, watching other people engage in certain behaviors makes it more acceptable for us to engage in those same activities.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.180
*Cough*  Logan Paul *Cough*  Ugh, excuse me.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:58.280
But in the case of tide pods, the sophisticated internet users knew that it was mostly meant as a joke.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.640
But, once it hit mainstream media, those people just started to do it because they thought it was real.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:03.240
**TRIGGER WARNING - suicide mentions until 8:34**

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:04.940
In fact, one area of heavy research right now

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.860
has featured sociologists looking into

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:08.860
how reporting teen suicides

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:14.280
actually causes suicide rates to increase. This applies locally as well as on the national level.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:20.680
When Marilyn Monroe's suicide was widely reported on, the national suicide rate increased by 12%.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:26.660
Because of this, many news organizations have policies in place against reporting teen suicides in obituaries.

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.240
In the process, these news organizations are acknowledging

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.800
that they have an ethical responsibility to protect the welfare of the public.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.860
Which kinda goes against what we are going to talk about next: How they choose their stories.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:44.380
Because all of this begs the question: Just how widespread of an epidemic was the Tide pod nonsense?

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:50.580
Earlier I had mentioned 86 total cases cited by poison control, which at first glance, seems like a lot.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.800
But to steal a line from PewDiePie, context matters!

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:59.400
Poison control centers across the U.S. receive MILLIONS of calls every single year.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:07.640
In 2016 alone, American poison control centers received 46,306 from parents concerned that

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:10.660
their kid had consumed too many vitamins?!

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:17.340
If 86 people eating a Tide Pod and calling poison control is cause for a news frenzy lasting for weeks,

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:24.200
then where the heck's the news story about the 46,306 kids chomping down on too many Flintstone's Chewables?!

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.160
Just looking at the numbers,

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:30.840
telling parents not to let their kids eat too many vitamins may seem like it would be the much more useful PSA

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.120
than having Gronk tell us:

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:36.000
[Gronk]: Use Tide Pods for washing, not eating.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.500
[MatPat]: Well, as it turns out, the fact that Tide Pod ingestion is so statistically insignificant

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:43.900
is actually a huge contributor to how it became such a big story.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:48.600
Research has shown that people respond most to news if it reports stories that are unique.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.120
Which actually makes sense when you stop and think about it, right?

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.720
The word 'new' is even in the name 'news'

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:58.860
This is why a crazy, murderous clown roaming through the woods is likely to make national headlines,

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:01.680
while deaths caused by traffic accidents just don't.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:08.000
In terms of actual danger to you, traffic accidents are a much bigger threat, but they just aren't newsworthy.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.940
They're an accepted part of everyday life.

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:12.960
Because people react to news stories based on novelty,

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:18.480
the actual prevalence of the issue, like Tide Pod poisoning, doesn't actually matter.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:22.200
In fact, the more common it is, the less likely it's going to get reported on!

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:25.780
That's why tens of thousands of vitamin overdoses get ignored,

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:30.000
while a handful of Tide Pod challenges are your top story for weeks.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.160
What's more, people are more likely to respond to news stories based on how threatening they sound.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.040
Research has shown that stories about non-threatening topics just don't get the

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:43.960
same kind of emotional response as stories informing people about *nasally voice* threats to their safety!

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.540
*normal voice* This is why reporting always slants negative.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:52.240
Reporting on weird dangers of the modern world is the most reliable way to keep an audience engaged.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:55.340
And what's weirder and more scary sounding to traditional media than

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:58.640
trends that teenagers are engaging with on social media?

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:01.400
Conveniently leave out the part that shows that the number of people

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.560
actually engaging in this behavior is minuscule,

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:06.000
and you've got yourself a viral news story and

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.520
a nation overreacting to something that wasn't really a big deal in the first place.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:15.580
And all us people who understand how to use the internet and internet culture suffer as a result.

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:21.480
This whole Tide Pod story, as dumb as it is, serves as an important reminder that now, more than ever,

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:27.060
media seems to be concerned, not with keeping us informed, but instead with keeping us engaged.

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:28.920
While it's absolutely fun to laugh at all the memes,

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.060
most of us missed out on the real story here.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:36.740
That this whole threat was basically a manufactured craze that, even at the height of it's popularity,

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:41.860
was barely even noticeable in terms of the total number of people it was actually affecting.

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:47.520
It also serves as a good reminder that directly lying to us isn't the only way for news to mislead us.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:52.480
It's actually possible for the media to tell stories that are 'technically, factually true',

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.160
while also giving us an inaccurate view of what's really going on in the world around us.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:02.840
The biggest bias in media isn't HOW stories are told, but WHICH stories are told.

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:07.960
For the last three years, I've been honored to partner with Bill and Melinda Gates to talk about their Annual Letter;

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:11.320
and this year's touches on these exact ideas.

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:15.580
If you listen to the headlines shouted on twitter or the stats tossed around on newspapers,

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:17.540
it seems like the world is in a terrible state.

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:21.120
"'Unconscionable': 7,000 babies die daily"

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:24.080
But if you read a bit deeper into the facts, what do you see?

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:26.960
"despite record low for child mortality".

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:30.920
The number of children who die every year has been cut literally IN HALF!

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:35.000
But that's not the fact that's making headlines here. It's not threatening enough.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:38.120
"Global poverty is declining but not fast enough"

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:39.580
Not fast enough, you say?

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:44.220
In just 20 years, humanitarian efforts have been able to decrease it by 50%!

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.500
How much faster do you want us to go? We're talking about the whole world here!

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:53.580
"By age 3, inequality is clear: Rich kids attend school. Poor kids stay with a grandparent".

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:57.780
Except, you left out the fact that more children are attending school now than ever before.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:03.100
In fact, people perceive things to be getting worse, even at the same time that they're actually becoming better!

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:08.960
For instance, this graph shows that the number of plane crashes has decreased over the last 25 year period,

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:13.440
but media coverage of plane crashes has increased in that same span of time.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.260
Yes, there is always room for improvement, and sure, we all wish that change would happen faster,

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:21.700
but it's important to remember that we live in an incredible time.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.140
A time for optimism.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:28.200
A time where good people are out there every single day working to make a positive difference in the lives of others.

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:30.600
I mean, the snow leopard is no longer endangered!

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.980
Honeybee populations are on the rise for, like, the first time ever!

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:37.580
Child labor is down 50% since the year 2000.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:41.300
HIV/AIDS is no longer the leading cause of death in Africa.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:47.040
And get this! A cancer vaccine has been proven effective in mice, and has now moved on to human testing!

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:53.240
I mean seriously! I couldn't believe this stuff either, but these are the true facts that don't get reported on every day.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:58.500
So click the link below to read Bill and Melinda Gate's letter and get a dose of positivity for a change.

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:00.500
It's a rare feeling these days, online;

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:03.080
but reading their letter was an incredible reminder that

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:06.920
hard work and love for your fellow man truly can make a difference.

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:09.440
Because in the end, the scariest news of them all is that

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:14.020
these positive stories are the ones that don't get reported.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.980
BUT HEY, THAT'S JUST A THEORY!

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:18.520
[Person]: No. Tada.

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:20.720
[MatPat]: And CUT!

